Done. `/ErrorBoundary.tsx` — here's what it provides:

**Core behavior**
- `getDerivedStateFromError` captures the error synchronously so the fallback renders immediately
- `componentDidCatch` stores `errorInfo` (component stack) and calls `console.error` with structured details
- `retry()` clears state so React re-mounts the children

**API**

| Prop | Type | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `children` | `ReactNode` | Tree to protect |
| `fallback` | `(FallbackProps) => ReactNode` | Custom fallback UI |
| `onError` | `(error, info) => void` | Hook to send to Sentry, etc. |

**Usage**

```tsx
// Basic
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary
  fallback={({ error, retry }) => (
    <div>
      <p>Oops: {error.message}</p>
      <button onClick={retry}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )}
  onError={(err, info) => Sentry.captureException(err, { extra: info })}
>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

One note: error boundaries only catch errors in **render / lifecycle / constructors** — not in event handlers or async code. For those, use `window.onerror` / `try/catch` separately.
